```python
from collections import defaultdict


class Solution:
    def longestSubarray(self, nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:
        n = len(nums)
        BIG = n + 1
        first = [BIG] * k
        last = [-1] * k
        s = 0
        first[0] = 0
        last[0] = 0
        for i, x in enumerate(nums, 1):
            s += x % k
            if s >= k:
                s -= k
            if first[s] == BIG:
                first[s] = i
            last[s] = i

        # Case A: subarray sum already divisible by k
        ans = 0
        for p in range(k):
            d = last[p] - first[p]
            if d > ans:
                ans = d

        # remainders that occur, sorted by first occurrence
        pairs = sorted((first[p], p) for p in range(k) if first[p] <= n)

        # witness positions grouped by v = (2*nums[j]) % k
        pos_by_v = defaultdict(list)
        for j, x in enumerate(nums):
            pos_by_v[(2 * x) % k].append(j)

        lst = last
        kk = k
        nn = n
        for v, pos in pos_by_v.items():
            m = len(pos)
            pt = 0
            bv = 0
            lim = nn - ans
            if lim <= 0:
                break
            for fp, p in pairs:
                if fp >= lim:
                    break
                while pt < m and pos[pt] < fp:
                    pt += 1
                if pt == m:
                    break
                q = p + v
                if q >= kk:
                    q -= kk
                lq = lst[q]
                if lq > fp and pos[pt] < lq:
                    c = lq - fp
                    if c > bv:
                        bv = c
            if bv > ans:
                ans = bv

        return ans if ans > 0 else 0
```

Wait: `if lim <= 0: break` — if ans == n, can't beat; break out of the whole v-loop. ✓ (lim = n - ans; need c = lq - fp > ans ≥ ...; c ≤ n - fp; need fp < n - ans = lim. So break when fp >= lim — candidates equal to ans don't matter since we only need max; using `>` vs `>=`: we skip candidates c ≤ ans — fine since ans already achieved. But careful: bv per-v then `if bv > ans`. Skipping c == ans is fine. ✓)

But there's subtlety: `lim` is computed at start of each v with current ans; as bv grows within v, could tighten, but not required.

Also `pairs` sorted ascending by fp; when multiple same fp, fine.

Also note: the while loop `while pt < m and pos[pt] < fp` — pt persists across p iterations (monotone fp). ✓ But careful: `pos[pt]` after loop is the smallest ≥ fp, correct only because previous fp' ≤ fp had pos[pt] ≥ fp'... Standard monotone two-pointer. ✓

